      "why": "no floor in characters: a word of one ideograph is found, because in a script with no spaces every place is an edge and the confinement does the sorting (« 門 » is held by one line of the term and by two of other keys)" },
    { "id": "term_evidence/of/a-capital-and-a-small-letter-are-one-word", "op": "of",
      "in": { "term": "Door", "lines": [
          {"key": "Front Door", "line": {"v": "Porte avant", "t": "V"}},
          {"key": "Door Lock", "line": {"v": "Verrou de porte", "t": "V"}} ] },
      "out": { "verdict": "word", "word": "Porte", "keys": ["Front Door", "Door Lock"], "rivals": [] },
      "why": "the first letter is read in either case, as everywhere a writing is looked for: one line has it capitalised and the other not, and each alone would be one line. Spelled the same number of times each way, the first line decides" },
    { "id": "term_evidence/of/a-block-half-of-whose-lines-are-not-the-terms-is-thin", "op": "of",
      "in": { "term": "Fire", "lines": [
          {"key": "Fire Pit", "line": {"v": "feu de camp et cuisson lente", "t": "V"}},
